Patch summary:
Stop ART from raising SIGSEGV that legacy game crash handlers steal, by making
every managed execution tier use explicit null checks, and by using explicit
ARM64 suspend checks.

ART normally generates deliberate hardware faults -- an `ldr` through a
protected suspend-trigger page, and loads through a null reference -- and
consumes the resulting signals in its own fault manager. Older
Breakpad/Crashpad-derived SDKs (NetEase CrashHunter and similar anti-cheat and
telemetry runtimes) hook `sigaction` with bytehook and install their handler
ahead of ART, so they see ART internal faults first, decide the signal is "not
throwable", and report it as a fatal native crash. What should have been a
catchable NullPointerException instead kills the process, or deadlocks it while
the handler ptrace-stops every thread to write a minidump.

Three tiers had to be covered, because a null receiver can be reached from any
of them:

  1. dex2oat  -- AOT and boot-image code.
  2. jit_compiler -- pinned explicit rather than inheriting the runtime flag.
  3. nterp    -- see below.

nterp did not null-check uniformly. Field access, array access and
invoke-direct/super already branch to `common_errNullObject`, but
invoke-virtual and invoke-interface deliberately dereferenced the receiver to
load its class and relied on the handler, as their own comment said: "Note: if
w1 is null, this will be handled by our SIGSEGV handler." Those four sites (two
per architecture) now use the same explicit check as the neighbouring
invoke-direct path. A null receiver in interpreted code was the single most
common path into this bug -- `Dialog.show` -> `PhoneWindow.installDecor` ->
`LayoutInflater.tryCreateView` reaches it during ordinary UI inflation.

IMPORTANT -- `implicit_null_checks_` stays enabled in Runtime::Init.
Clearing it stops NullPointerHandler from being installed, which is only safe
if nothing can still fault. It is deliberately left on so the handler remains
as a backstop; the safe direction is explicit-check code with the handler
present, and the unsafe direction is implicit-check code with no handler. That
combination takes down ordinary system apps (Settings, Phonesky, Wellbeing)
with `SEGV_MAPERR fault addr 0x0` in `nterp_op_invoke_virtual`. See also the
comment on `fault_manager.AddGeneratedCodeRange(nterp_header...)`: "Nterp code
can use signal handling just like the compiled managed code."

Implicit ARM64 suspend checks are disabled in both the runtime and dex2oat.
That pair is consistent on its own and has shipped previously.

Cost: one compare-and-branch per dereference in compiled code and per receiver
in nterp invoke-virtual/interface, and FastCompiler::CompileARM64 bails out
when implicit null checks are off, so JIT baseline compilation falls back to
the optimizing compiler.

Keep native-bridge loader namespaces from advertising APK-embedded native
library paths. The x86_64 desktop image can run ARM64 apps through the native
bridge, but translated guest linkers are much more reliable when the selected
libraries are real extracted files instead of `apk.zip!/lib/...` paths.

This patch leaves the normal native app namespace behavior alone and only
filters search paths for bridged namespaces. The intent is to make translated
apps load predictably without weakening namespace isolation for regular apps.

diff --git a/compiler/jit/jit_compiler.cc b/compiler/jit/jit_compiler.cc
index 6b72727641..e8ec306765 100644
--- a/compiler/jit/jit_compiler.cc
+++ b/compiler/jit/jit_compiler.cc
@@ -78,7 +78,12 @@ void JitCompiler::ParseCompilerOptions() {
     compiler_options_->SetDebuggable(runtime->IsJavaDebuggable());
   }
 
-  compiler_options_->implicit_null_checks_ = runtime->GetImplicitNullChecks();
+  // Emit explicit null checks in JIT-compiled code. The runtime still installs
+  // the implicit-check fault handler for nterp, but compiled managed code must
+  // not raise SIGSEGV of its own: legacy game crash handlers (NetEase
+  // CrashHunter and other Breakpad derivatives) hook sigaction ahead of ART and
+  // report ART internal faults as native crashes.
+  compiler_options_->implicit_null_checks_ = false;
   compiler_options_->implicit_so_checks_ = runtime->GetImplicitStackOverflowChecks();
   compiler_options_->implicit_suspend_checks_ = runtime->GetImplicitSuspendChecks();
 
diff --git a/dex2oat/dex2oat.cc b/dex2oat/dex2oat.cc
index d279e93397..72515d08e7 100644
--- a/dex2oat/dex2oat.cc
+++ b/dex2oat/dex2oat.cc
@@ -849,14 +849,12 @@ class Dex2Oat final {
     // Set the compilation target's implicit checks options.
     switch (compiler_options_->GetInstructionSet()) {
       case InstructionSet::kArm64:
-        compiler_options_->implicit_suspend_checks_ = true;
         FALLTHROUGH_INTENDED;
       case InstructionSet::kArm:
       case InstructionSet::kThumb2:
       case InstructionSet::kRiscv64:
       case InstructionSet::kX86:
       case InstructionSet::kX86_64:
-        compiler_options_->implicit_null_checks_ = true;
         compiler_options_->implicit_so_checks_ = true;
         break;
 
